<score-partwise version="4.0">
  <work><work-title>Engraving fixture: independent voices, cross-staff voices, and chords (R-2 B5/B6)</work-title></work>
  <identification>
    <creator type="arranger">Musicanyya test fixture</creator>
    <rights>CC0-1.0 (test fixture, original work)</rights>
    <encoding><software>Musicanyya</software><encoding-date>2026-09-23</encoding-date></encoding>
  </identification>
  <part-list>
    <score-part id="P1"><part-name>Piano</part-name></score-part>
  </part-list>
  <part id="P1">
  <!--
  Feature 006 fixture: two-staff piano part (<staves>2</staves>), divisions=4 (eighth=2, sixteenth=1
  division units). No <beam> element is present anywhere in this file.

  M1 (4/4) - two voices sharing the SAME staff (both <staff>1</staff>), each filling the bar completely
  and independently (voice 1 plain eighths, voice 2 plain sixteenths), to show that beat-grouping is
  computed per (part, voice) - B6 - not per staff.
    Voice 1 = eighths C4 D4 E4 F4 (half 1) G4 A4 B4 C5 (half 2), all plain/non-tuplet. B4 4/4 exception:
    each half-bar has exactly four plain eighths -> each half is its own ONE group. Expected: a 4-note
    beam over C4-D4-E4-F4, and a SEPARATE 4-note beam over G4-A4-B4-C5 (grouping never crosses the
    bar's middle).
    Voice 2 = sixteenths C5 D5 E5 F5 | G5 F5 E5 D5 | C5 D5 E5 F5 | G5 F5 E5 D5, one group of four per
    quarter (B4's half-bar exception only applies to EIGHTH notes, not sixteenths, so the default
    per-quarter rule, B3, applies here). Expected: FOUR separate 4-note beams, each with a continuous
    level-1 (eighth) beam AND a continuous level-2 (sixteenth) beam (begin-continue-continue-end at both
    levels - every note in each group of four has a same-level neighbour, so no hooks anywhere in this
    voice). Voice 1's two beams and voice 2's four beams are entirely independent of each other even
    though they occupy the identical staff and the identical bar.

  M2 (4/4) - a SINGLE voice (voice 1 throughout) whose notes are split across <staff>1</staff> and
  <staff>2</staff> within that one voice - a cross-staff run, common in piano writing for a beamed
  passage that dips onto the other staff. Eight plain eighths: half 1 = C5(staff1) D5(staff1) G3(staff2)
  A3(staff2); half 2 = B3(staff2) C4(staff2) F5(staff1) G5(staff1). Each half-bar holds exactly four
  plain, non-tuplet eighths -> B4's 4/4 exception makes each half ONE group, regardless of the staff
  each note is printed on (B6: "staff is ignored for grouping purposes"). Expected: a 4-note beam over
  C5-D5-G3-A3 that spans staff 1 into staff 2 mid-beam, and a SEPARATE 4-note beam over B3-C4-F5-G5 that
  spans staff 2 into staff 1 mid-beam - both are single beams of ONE voice, not two per-staff beams.

  M3 (2/4) - a beam-eligible chord. Beat 1 = a three-note eighth chord (C4 base note + E4 + G4, the
  latter two carrying <chord/>) followed by a plain eighth D4 - two beat-1 events (the chord counts as
  ONE event per B5), filling the quarter exactly. Beat 2 = a plain filler quarter F4 (not beam-eligible).
  Expected: a 2-note primary beam over [chord, D4]. Per the spec ("beams are written on the chord head,
  the note without <chord/>"), the beam is placed on C4 (begin) and D4 (end); E4 and G4 - the <chord/>
  members - carry no beam element of their own.
  -->
  <measure number="1">
